ses help them out provide some  additional relief from the department  defense you know mr. secretary hear you  say you're proud of them but it's always  interesting to me they're proud to do it  they want to do this they want to get  out there and help and risk their lives  that's the definition of a true hero to  me yeah they really do and many of them  if you look at the National Guard who  were the first to deploy we have over  30,000 now deployed across the United  States and they're coming  from there's those homes and communities  and then as we've had the deep start  reaching into our reserve forces we've  actually had many doctors nurses and  others just volunteer to come back on  active duty or volunteer for active duty  to serve so again I'm very proud of them  they have my highest respect and regard  for what they're doing today across the  country  well the biggest outbreak that we're  hearing is on the aircraft carrier the  Theodore Roosevelt there's 1,900  servicemen overall but 400 are on the  aircraft the Theodore Roosevelt what  steps are being done to protect the  servicemen there and on other in other  places yes so we have a less than 10  percent of the crew is infected and all  but one are mild to moderate cases so I  think the Navy is taking a lot of  prudent steps to get that under control  the most important thing right now is to  take care of our sailors make sure  they're getting the care they needed and  and then get the ship back to sea  so it can perform its its wartime  mission and like I said the Navy's been  pushing supplies out there doctors we've  been able to get thousands of the  sailors off the ship and taking care of  ashore and I've spoken with the Acting  Secretary the Navy and the Chief of  Naval Operations and I know they're  doing everything they can to make sure  we accomplish those two missions and  talking about the acting Navy secretary  the Bret Crozier resignation has been  widely discussed you have certainly been  a